![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The testbench comment states: 'Verify by checking it takes the same number of cycles to spike again from a fresh start.' However, the implementation only checks `spike_out == 1'b0` once and immediately finishes without actually driving `spike_in` again to verify the spike timing. The testbench should be extended to actually perform this verification.

```
        spike_in = 1'b0;
        @(negedge clk);
        check(spike_out == 1'b0, "spike_out should stay low while membrane is reset and decaying");

        // Actually verify it takes the same number of cycles to spike again
        spike_in = 1'b1;
        @(negedge clk); // mem: 0 -> 40
        @(negedge clk); // mem: 40 -> 79
        @(negedge clk); // mem: 79 -> 118 -> crosses threshold(100)
        check(spike_out == 1'b1, "spike_out should assert again after 3 cycles of integration");
```

![critical](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/critical.svg)

There are two critical issues in this hold buffer implementation:

1. **Incorrect Reset Signal**: The block is sensitive to `negedge rst_n` and checks `if (!rst_n)`. However, `rst_n` is the raw active-high button input from the pin (BTNC). This means the hold buffer will be held in reset constantly during normal operation (when the button is not pressed). It should use the active-low inverted reset signal `rst` instead.
2. **Hold Pending Override Bug**: When `rx_valid` is asserted and `hold_pending && !tx_busy` is also true, the assignment `hold_pending <= 1'b0` in the second `if` block overrides `hold_pending <= 1'b1` from the first `if` block. This causes the newly received byte in `tx_hold` to be lost because `hold_pending` is incorrectly cleared to `0`.

The suggested code resolves both issues by using `rst` and structuring the conditional blocks to correctly handle simultaneous transmission and reception.

```
    always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst) begin
        if (!rst) begin
            tx_hold      <= '0;
            hold_pending <= 1'b0;
            tx_data      <= '0;
            tx_send      <= 1'b0;
        end else begin
            tx_send <= 1'b0;
            if (rx_valid) begin
                tx_hold      <= rx_data;
                hold_pending <= 1'b1;
                if (hold_pending && !tx_busy) begin
                    tx_data      <= tx_hold;
                    tx_send      <= 1'b1;
                end
            end else begin
                if (hold_pending && !tx_busy) begin
                    tx_data      <= tx_hold;
                    tx_send      <= 1'b1;
                    hold_pending <= 1'b0;
                end
            end
        end
    end
```
